1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,037
EverLink is an open-source, self-hosted link-rot steward.

2
00:00:04,037 --> 00:00:08,062
Checkers only report — EverLink decides, and you approve.

3
00:00:08,062 --> 00:00:12,762
These are my three content sites: twenty-three thousand outbound link slots.

4
00:00:12,762 --> 00:00:15,375
The last time every one was checked by hand?

5
00:00:15,375 --> 00:00:16,125
Never.

6
00:00:16,125 --> 00:00:20,450
They rot a little every day, and nobody sees it until a reader does.

7
00:00:20,450 --> 00:00:22,000
So an agent patrols them.

8
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,550
Who it's for: independent publishers and small content teams — we're entering the Professional Agents track.

9
00:00:28,550 --> 00:00:35,600
Why an agent: decide-and-escalate is repetitive, judgment-heavy work — exactly what an agent should take on.

10
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:37,737
The principle in three lines.

11
00:00:37,737 --> 00:00:40,800
Patrol: every outbound link, every night.

12
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:48,262
Decide: a real LLM Judge on Amazon Bedrock drafts the fix, constrained by four Steering policies.

13
00:00:48,262 --> 00:00:55,175
Act and prove: apply, re-verify, roll back on failure — every step in a public audit trail.

14
00:00:55,175 --> 00:00:59,962
Open source under MIT: github dot tomyuya slash everlink.

15
00:00:59,962 --> 00:01:03,712
And a live example: everlink-seven dot vercel dot app.

16
00:01:03,712 --> 00:01:07,650
How you use it: clone it, deploy it on your own infrastructure.

17
00:01:07,650 --> 00:01:09,100
Links get in two ways.

18
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:16,600
Path A: a generic read-only crawl of any website — no database, no credentials, never writes back.

19
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:23,750
Path B: a read-only snapshot of your own database, unlocking block-level fixes and gated write-back.

20
00:01:23,750 --> 00:01:27,550
Your source database stays strictly read-only either way.

21
00:01:27,550 --> 00:01:36,337
Demo one: Path B, the full loop on my own site — the nightly cron's loop, run by hand, dry-run, so nothing is written.

22
00:01:36,337 --> 00:01:42,437
I type: everlink scan, site aethelgem, judge mantle, dry-run, limit six.

23
00:01:42,437 --> 00:01:56,287
A Scanner agent pulls each link slot from my database snapshot and probes it — L1 HTTP status and redirect-chain analysis first, then an L2 stealthy page parse only where L1 is inconclusive.

24
00:01:56,287 --> 00:02:07,712
A Judge agent — a real LLM on Amazon Bedrock — reads that evidence and drafts a structured Proposal: replace this URL, rewrite that anchor, or escalate.

25
00:02:07,712 --> 00:02:14,675
Anything blocked or timed out is flagged needs-human-recheck: the agent knows its limits and never fabricates.

26
00:02:14,675 --> 00:02:19,925
The scan report lands: real proposals scrolling, each with its evidence chain.

27
00:02:19,925 --> 00:02:25,975
Then the morning brief: sixteen problem slots, pushed to me as a notification, not an app.

28
00:02:25,975 --> 00:02:29,087
The only screen I open: the decision inbox.

29
00:02:29,087 --> 00:02:33,687
Sixteen cards wait, each with its evidence and its proposed fix.

30
00:02:33,687 --> 00:02:38,412
I approve the safe ones in a batch; I read the risky ones individually.

31
00:02:38,412 --> 00:02:45,175
Rejections carry a typed why — and the agent remembers it, so it won't re-propose the same fix next week.

32
00:02:45,175 --> 00:02:46,762
Two guards matter most.

33
00:02:46,762 --> 00:02:55,675
This disclosure block is dead — but deleting it is a legal disaster, so a Steering policy cancels the drop and the audit logs steering-cancel.

34
00:02:55,675 --> 00:03:04,112
And when I approve a fix, the Writer snapshots the block, applies in one transaction, then re-probes the new link to prove it's alive.

35
00:03:04,112 --> 00:03:08,350
If verification fails, it rolls back and dead-letters the decision.

36
00:03:08,350 --> 00:03:12,587
Last night: thirty-seven links healed, verified back to zero.

37
00:03:12,587 --> 00:03:15,512
And I don't ask you to trust the agent — I test it.

38
00:03:15,512 --> 00:03:26,200
A fifty-case eval suite runs offline against known ground truth: detection one hundred percent, steering violations zero, three hard metrics at one hundred percent.

39
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:28,775
And the oracle is proven non-vacuous.

40
00:03:28,775 --> 00:03:32,575
Demo two: Path A, the generic read-only crawl.

41
00:03:32,575 --> 00:03:36,075
No database, no code change, no credentials.

42
00:03:36,075 --> 00:03:45,312
I point it at a site EverLink has never seen: everlink scan, site blog dot python dot org, include-internal, dry-run, limit eight.

43
00:03:45,312 --> 00:03:46,300
Watch it work.

44
00:03:46,300 --> 00:04:06,912
It honours robots.txt, fetches the sitemap — and if that's an index, it says so and expands one — crawls a few pages, extracts every outbound link, skips social-share and same-site links as noise, then probes each survivor over read-only HTTP: at most one request per link, politely rate-limited.

45
00:04:06,912 --> 00:04:12,550
No LLM, no credentials, and it never writes back — not here, not anywhere.

46
00:04:12,650 --> 00:04:23,550
The payoff: a plain link-rot report in the terminal — every outbound slot with its HTTP verdict, a healthy count, and the honesty line: read-only, nothing written.

47
00:04:23,550 --> 00:04:29,987
That is the whole point of Path A: any website, a real report in under a minute, zero setup.

48
00:04:29,987 --> 00:04:34,537
Path B goes deeper — block-level fixes and write-back on your own site.

49
00:04:34,537 --> 00:04:37,800
Path A is the door; Path B is the house.

50
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:41,500
EverLink patrols twenty-three thousand links so I don't have to.

51
00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:49,337
It's honest about its limits, never touches my disclosures, verifies its own fixes, and hands me back about six hours a week.

52
00:04:49,337 --> 00:04:54,637
Built with the Strands Agents SDK and Amazon Bedrock for Agents for Humans.

53
00:04:54,637 --> 00:04:57,125
You approve decisions, not links.

